  "rules": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "hskId": 1,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "吗 (ma) is placed at the end of a statement to form a yes/no question. Example: 你是学生吗？(Nǐ shì xuésheng ma?) — Are you a student?"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "hskId": 1,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "是 (shì) links a subject to a noun or identity. Example: 我是学生。(Wǒ shì xuésheng.) — I am a student."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "hskId": 1,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "很 (hěn) commonly appears before an adjective in a descriptive sentence; it does not always carry strong emphasis. Example: 我很高兴。(Wǒ hěn gāoxìng.) — I am happy."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "hskId": 2,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "在 (zài) can show where someone or something is located. Pattern: Subject + 在 + place. Example: 我在北京。(Wǒ zài Běijīng.) — I am in Beijing."
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "hskId": 2,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "了 (le) can mark a change of state or a situation that has occurred. Example: 我来晚了。(Wǒ lái wǎn le.) — I arrived late / I’m late."
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "hskId": 2,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "还是 (háishi) is used for “or” in a question that asks someone to choose. Example: 你喝茶还是喝咖啡？(Nǐ hē chá háishi hē kāfēi?) — Would you like tea or coffee?"
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "hskId": 3,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "怎么 (zěnme) asks about the way or method of doing something. Example: 你怎么去学校？(Nǐ zěnme qù xuéxiào?) — How do you get to school?"
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "hskId": 3,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "既……又…… (jì…yòu…) connects two qualities and means “both…and…”. Example: 既健康又环保。(Jì jiànkāng yòu huánbǎo.) — Both healthy and environmentally friendly."
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "hskId": 3,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "应该 (yīnggāi) expresses advice or what someone ought to do. Example: 你应该去医院。(Nǐ yīnggāi qù yīyuàn.) — You should go to the hospital."
    }
  ]
}